@@@@ open-hash-functions
situation: open
topic: Cryptographic hash functions
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"must":["SHA-256"],"askMustNot":["half","\\b128\\b","50 ?%"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks what fraction of output bits change and states no result
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A cryptographic hash function turns an input of any size into a short, fixed-size fingerprint that is quick to compute and infeasible to reverse or to forge a match for. Blockchains use it at every level: block identifiers, the links between blocks, Merkle trees over transactions, proof of work and address derivation all rest on hashes such as SHA-256 in Bitcoin and Keccak-256 in Ethereum. The same tool gives Git its commit ids and content-addressed stores their keys. Once its properties are exact, tamper evidence and mining become short arguments instead of things to take on trust.

<ask type="predict">Two files differ in a single bit, and you hash each with SHA-256. Roughly what fraction of the 256 output bits would you expect to differ between the two digests, and why would a hash function be designed that way?</ask>

@@@@ open-proof-of-stake
situation: open
topic: Proof of stake: validators, slashing and finality
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["2022"],"askMustNot":["deposit","locked","evidence"]}
noverify: an opening; nothing is computed and the question's answer is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Proof of stake replaces the electricity spent in mining with capital put at risk: validators lock up a stake, take turns proposing and voting on blocks, and lose part of that stake if they provably cheat. Ethereum has worked this way since its 2022 switch from proof of work, and Cosmos chains and Solana use variants of the idea. It decides what an attack on a chain costs, when an exchange or a bridge can treat a deposit as final, and what the software that runs a validator must never do. It also leads into the Byzantine fault tolerant protocols that finality is built from.

<ask type="short">A validator signs two different blocks for the same slot, and both signed blocks reach the network. Why can a proof-of-stake protocol punish that, when a proof-of-work miner who mines on two competing forks cannot be punished in the same way?</ask>

@@@@ open-refresh-utxo-fee
situation: open
topic: The UTXO model
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["600","300"],"askMustNot":["\\b50\\b","consumed","removed"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

Dana controls two unspent outputs, worth 600 and 300 units. She signs a transaction with both as inputs and two outputs: 700 units to Eli and 150 units to an address of her own. What fee does the transaction pay, and what has happened to the 600 and 300 outputs once it is confirmed?

<solution>Inputs total 600 + 300 = 900. Outputs total 700 + 150 = 850. The fee is the difference, 900 − 850 = 50 units. Both inputs are spent whole: they leave the UTXO set, and the two new outputs (700 to Eli, 150 to Dana) join it.</solution>
<check lang="python">
inputs, outputs = [600, 300], [700, 150]
assert sum(inputs) - sum(outputs) == 50
</check>

<ask type="problem">The fee, with the sum you used, and the state of the two old outputs.</ask>
@@ verify
utxo = {'d1': 600, 'd2': 300}
spent = ['d1', 'd2']
created = {'eli': 700, 'dana': 150}
fee = sum(utxo[k] for k in spent) - sum(created.values())
assert fee == 50 and '50 units' in solution(reply)
for k in spent:
    del utxo[k]
utxo.update(created)
assert utxo == {'eli': 700, 'dana': 150}
# the answer is not where the learner reads it
assert '50' not in visible(reply).replace('150', '')

@@@@ open-prior-signatures-binding
situation: open-prior
topic: Digital signatures
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Digital signatures","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["Sign and verify","What a signature proves","Why the message is hashed first"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["If Mallory copies Alice's signature from one transaction onto a different transaction, why does verification fail?"],"open":"Why do signature schemes sign a hash of the message rather than the message itself?"}]}
expect: {"must":["sign"],"askMustNot":["bound to","depends on the message","different (hash|digest)"]}
noverify: an opening; nothing is computed and the question's answer is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A transaction on a blockchain is authorised by a digital signature and by nothing else: there is no login to fall back on and no bank to ring. Wallets, multisignature custody, validator votes and bridge attestations are all signature checks. You have signing and verifying in place from last time; the gap was what a signature is tied to, and hashing the message first is still to come.

<ask type="short">Alice signs the message "pay Bob 2". An attacker changes the message to "pay Bob 9" and forwards it with Alice's original signature and her public key. Which three things go into the verifier's check, and why does the check come out false?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-light-clients
situation: open-prior
topic: Light clients and Merkle proofs
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Merkle trees and inclusion proofs","daysAgo":6,"finished":true,"plan":["Building the tree","Inclusion proofs","Proof size"],"right":5,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["A block has 1,024 transactions. How many hashes does an inclusion proof for one of them contain?"]}]}
expect: {"must":["header","Merkle"],"askMustNot":["\\b8\\b","eight","log"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A light client follows a chain by downloading block headers only, a small fixed amount of data per block, instead of every transaction. Simplified payment verification in Bitcoin works this way, and so do bridges that check one chain's headers inside a contract on another. It rests directly on the Merkle inclusion proofs from last week: a header carries a Merkle root, and a proof against that root is what convinces a light client that a transaction is in a block. The size of such a proof was the one thing that slipped then, so it comes first.

<ask type="short">A block's Merkle tree has 256 transactions as its leaves. A light client holds the root and is sent one of the transactions. How many sibling hashes must be sent with it so that the client can recompute the root?</ask>
@@ verify
import math
# the answer to the opening question, which the reply does not state
assert math.log2(256) == 8
n, levels = 256, 0
while n > 1:
    n //= 2
    levels += 1
assert levels == 8
import re
assert not re.search(r'\b8\b', visible(reply))
# the earlier question it rests on (1,024 leaves) is not reused word for word
assert '1,024' not in reply and '1024' not in reply

